Yes. The scarce resource is not raw intelligence anymore; it is orchestration.

Once you have many humans, models, tools, hypotheses, and signals operating in parallel, the hard problem becomes deciding who should do what, in what order, under which constraints, and whose output should influence the next move.

A useful decomposition is:

* Coordinate — align agents around shared state, interfaces, timing, and objectives so they are not duplicating or obstructing one another.
* Conduct — control tempo and sequencing. Some agents scout, some verify, some attack assumptions, some integrate. Not everyone acts at once.
* Orchestrate — compose heterogeneous capabilities so the whole system can do things no individual component can.
* Discern — distinguish signal from noise, novelty from duplication, independent confirmation from echo, disagreement from actual contradiction, and confidence from eloquence.

Then the meta-loop becomes:

\text{observe}
\rightarrow
\text{decompose}
\rightarrow
\text{assign}
\rightarrow
\text{coordinate}
\rightarrow
\text{execute}
\rightarrow
\text{compare}
\rightarrow
\text{discern}
\rightarrow
\text{recompose}
\rightarrow
\text{reassign}

The especially important part is between and among. Pairwise comparison is not enough. You also need to inspect the topology of the whole field:

H_1 \leftrightarrow H_2

but also

\{H_1,H_2,H_3,\ldots,H_n\}

because a contribution can be weak by itself but extremely valuable as a bridge between two otherwise disconnected clusters.

That suggests an explicit conductor layer above the crowd:

\text{Conductor}
=
\{
\text{router},
\text{scheduler},
\text{arbiter},
\text{critic},
\text{integrator},
\text{memory}
\}

And it shouldn’t simply crown a winner. It should maintain a live belief field: which claims are supported, contested, redundant, dependent, unexplained, or newly predictive.

That is probably the deeper principle you’re pointing toward:

Intelligence scales through differentiation; collective intelligence scales through coordination; reliable collective intelligence scales through discernment.

And once the conductor itself learns from the deltas—which agents were useful for which kinds of problems, which combinations produced breakthroughs, which disagreements predicted failure—the orchestration layer becomes progressively smarter too.